WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:04.790
It's no secret that Japanese animation can
go to some pretty weird places… but just

00:00:04.790 --> 00:00:06.940
how strange does it really get?

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:10.809
From experimental short films to psychedelic
roller coasters, here's a look at some of

00:00:10.809 --> 00:00:13.690
the most bizarre Japanese anime ever made.

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:15.199
Paprika

00:00:15.199 --> 00:00:20.750
Based on the 1993 novel of the same name,
2006's Paprika centers around Doctor Atsuko

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:24.970
Chiba, a research psychologist who begins
testing a groundbreaking new psychotherapy

00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:29.820
tool that allows users to enter people's subconscious
minds to get glimpses at their dreams and

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:31.759
rewire their personalities.

00:00:31.759 --> 00:00:34.070
When the tech is hijacked, things go haywire.

00:00:34.070 --> 00:00:38.660
We're talking building-leaping, skin-peeling,
body-morphing crazy, in a surreal setting

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:43.690
filled with larger-than-life creatures, half-human
hybrids, and walking refrigerators.

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:47.660
It's a cautionary tale about meddling with
the minds of mankind, told through a deeply

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:51.940
bizarre, nightmarish lens that erases the
line between real life and pretend.

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:56.240
Critics have called Paprika an "eye-opening
mind trip" not meant to be understood, a film

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:59.890
that rarely makes sense for all its unsettling
elements.

00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:00.920
Cat Soup

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:04.900
Cross anthropomorphic kittens and a creepy
sort-of coming-of-age tale, and you've got

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:05.930
Cat Soup.

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:11.060
Inspired by the works of manga artist Nekojiru
and directed by Tatsuo Satō, this bite-sized

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:16.640
anime from 2001 follows the relationship between
two tiny, intelligent cats — Nyāko, who

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:21.610
is extremely ill, and Nyatta, who's rendered
clinically deceased after a tragic accident.

00:01:21.610 --> 00:01:25.750
Not willing to give up on his dying sister,
Nyatta embarks on an engrossing, grotesque

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:29.240
voyage to rescue Nyāko's soul from a Buddha-like
figure.

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:34.030
The pair swap bodies, split souls, shove their
hands inside a giant pig, and take part in

00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:37.340
a Noah's Ark scenario in their spirit-searching
journey.

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:42.030
Think of it like Hello Kitty mixed with Salvador
Dali… which is to say that it's very, very

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:42.560
strange.

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:46.760
Interstella 5555

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:50.460
If the full name of this anime alone didn't
tip you off that this is something else, what

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:56.380
goes down in Interstella 5555: The 5tory of
the 5ecret 5tar 5ystem will boggle your brain.

00:01:56.380 --> 00:02:00.760
Directed by Kazuhisa Takenouchi, known for
his work on Dragon Ball, this anime can best

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:03.049
be billed as a Daft Punk Fantasia.

00:02:03.049 --> 00:02:08.229
It has no dialogue, just retro-inspired visuals
done by Toei Animation, set to an electro-punk

00:02:08.229 --> 00:02:09.229
soundtrack.

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:14.019
While Interstella 5555 is essentially a music
video accompaniment to Daft Punk's 2001 album

00:02:14.019 --> 00:02:18.639
Discovery, it actually tells the pretty strange
story of a rag-tag band of alien musicians

00:02:18.639 --> 00:02:22.629
who get abducted and shipped off to Earth
to play for legions of adoring fans — but

00:02:22.629 --> 00:02:23.819
there's a catch.

00:02:23.819 --> 00:02:28.319
Skin-altering procedures, species exploitation,
and a rogue pilot who will go to the end of

00:02:28.319 --> 00:02:33.090
the universe to save the band's female member
await you in Interstella 5555, the anime that

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:36.680
plays out like a techno-driven fever dream
you won't ever forget.

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:39.129
GYO: Tokyo Fish Attack

00:02:39.129 --> 00:02:43.709
The title of 2012's Tokyo Fish Attack pretty
much explains it all: fish rise from the sea

00:02:43.709 --> 00:02:46.810
on shiny metal legs to terrorize the city
of Tokyo.

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:50.930
As the film runs on, waves of increasingly
dangerous sea creatures come crashing to the

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:51.930
shore.

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:57.249
Prepare for incredibly cheesy romance scenes,
spooky circus people, power-hungry mad scientists,

00:02:57.249 --> 00:03:00.760
mutants with bathroom-based powers, and a
squid that may make you never want to eat

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:01.760
seafood again.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:05.870
GYO: Tokyo Fish Attack, which is based on
a horror manga series, has its fair share

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:09.310
of gory moments, but the violence doesn't
seem too serious when you remember that the

00:03:09.310 --> 00:03:12.450
bad guys are walking fish.

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:13.450
Mind Game

00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:18.270
The weird work of writer and director Masaaki
Yuasa, Mind Game is the story of Nishi, a

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:22.000
young man who dreams of one day becoming a
comic book artist and winning the heart of

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:23.659
his childhood crush.

00:03:23.659 --> 00:03:28.999
After the two have a brush with the Japanese
mafia, Nishi gets trapped in a spiritual limbo.

00:03:28.999 --> 00:03:33.599
Filled to the top with mind-bending existential
themes and unusual animation, Mind Game soars

00:03:33.599 --> 00:03:37.760
through Nishi's wild journey back to the living
world on a road marked with giant whales,

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:42.780
caricatured villains, and neon car chases,
which is as cool as it is bizarre.

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:44.279
Dead Leaves

00:03:44.279 --> 00:03:49.300
This 2004 sci-fi anime tells the tale of two
unlikely rebels, Retro and Pandy, who are

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:52.920
shipped off to prison after committing an
insane string of crimes.

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:56.139
While locked up, the pair meet some out-there
characters, and have their patience put to

00:03:56.139 --> 00:03:57.139
the test.

00:03:57.139 --> 00:03:59.699
The story only gets weirder as the plot unwinds.

00:03:59.699 --> 00:04:04.239
Without spoiling the madcap twist, Retro and
Pandy's post-prison adventures see them encountering

00:04:04.239 --> 00:04:09.589
an oversized demon caterpillar and a mutant
baby, crash-landing onto a dystopian Earth,

00:04:09.589 --> 00:04:12.180
and possibly breaking the space-time continuum.

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:13.669
How's that for bizarre?

00:04:13.669 --> 00:04:14.790
Tekkonkinkreet

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:19.819
The title of Tekkonkinkreet translates from
Japanese to English as "reinforced concrete,"

00:04:19.819 --> 00:04:24.419
and that concept holds true to the central
vision and underlying moral tale of this anime.

00:04:24.419 --> 00:04:28.510
An adaptation of a manga series, this film
surrounds penniless orphan brothers Kuro and

00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:31.789
Shiro in sights and sounds that dizzy and
dazzle the viewer.

00:04:31.789 --> 00:04:35.139
The pair rob their way through Treasure Town
without much trouble — until the Yakuza

00:04:35.139 --> 00:04:36.229
enter the scene.

00:04:36.229 --> 00:04:40.960
Upon the arrival of the notorious Japanese
gang, Tekkonkinkreet devolves into a disturbing,

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:45.509
hypnotic string of scenes that flip-flop between
true crimes and the trippy world of Shiro's

00:04:45.509 --> 00:04:46.509
mind.

00:04:46.509 --> 00:04:49.930
As with a few other films here, Tekkonkinkreet
will move you, but not before shaking you

00:04:49.930 --> 00:04:52.729
to your core with its unsettling strangeness.

00:04:52.729 --> 00:04:54.479
Angel's Egg

00:04:54.479 --> 00:04:59.319
Released in 1985, Angel's Egg tells the story
of a young girl who lives alone in a lair

00:04:59.319 --> 00:05:03.540
in an abandoned city, where she serves as
the sole protector of an enormous egg that

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:05.479
apparently holds great value.

00:05:05.479 --> 00:05:09.539
After a mysterious boy joins her, the two
witness several mystical happenings involving

00:05:09.539 --> 00:05:13.690
orbs, goddesses, and angels, and chat briefly
about philosophy and theology.

00:05:13.690 --> 00:05:17.939
Though the film clocks in at around 80 minutes,
not much of its runtime is comprised of actual

00:05:17.939 --> 00:05:18.939
dialogue.

00:05:18.939 --> 00:05:23.240
Instead, the avant-garde anime leans on a
wispy plot and heavy, abnormal visuals, doubling

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:25.629
down in the final "act" to reveal a cryptic
half-resolution.

00:05:25.629 --> 00:05:28.560
So...y'know...don't forget the popcorn!

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:29.629
Thanks for watching!

00:05:29.629 --> 00:05:32.009
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:32.009 --> 00:05:34.110
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

